  "topic_en": "Relations between the coefficients of a polynomial equation and the power sums of its roots (Newton's identities), with a worked example of a quartic and a general formula for the n-th power sum.",
  "transcription": "Sit data aequatio\n$x^n - Ax^{n-1} + Bx^{n-2} - Cx^{n-3} + \\ldots\\ldots = 0$\ncuius [-et-] radices $x', x'', x''' \\ldots x^N$\nsit\n$\\Sigma x = (1)$\n$\\Sigma xx = (2)$\n$\\Sigma x^3 = (3)$\n& sic deinceps : tum\n$(a^7b^7) = (a^6b^6)(ab)$\n$- (a^7b^6c) - (a^6b^6cd)$\n$(a^7b^7) = a^7b^6$\n$A = (1)$\n$B = \\frac{1}{2}(1)^2 - \\frac{1}{2}(2)$\n$C = \\frac{1}{6}(1)^3 - \\frac{1}{2}(1)(2) + \\frac{1}{3}(3)$\n$D = \\frac{1}{24}(1)^4 - \\frac{1}{4}(1)^2(2) + \\frac{1}{8}(2)^2 + \\frac{1}{3}(1)(3) - \\frac{1}{4}(4)$\nSit E.g. $(1) = -1$ ; $(2) = -3$ ; $(3) = +17$ ; $(4) = -27$\nErit aequatio fundam.\n$x^4 + x^3 + 2xx - 4x + 3 = 0$\n(Obiter $= (xx + \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{5}{2})^2 - 13(\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2})^2$)\n$(1) = A$\n$(2) = AA - 2B$\n$(3) = A^3 - 3AB + 3C$\n$(4) = A^4 - 4AAB + 2B^2 + 4AC - 4D$\n$(5) = A^5 - 5A^3B + 5AB^2 + 5AAC - 5BC - 5AD + 5E$\n$(6) = A^6 - 6A^4B + 9A^2B^2 - 2B^3 + 6A^3C + 3CC - 12ABC \\ldots$\n$- 6AAD + 6AE - 6F$\n$+ 6BD$\n$(n) = A^n - nA^{n-2}B + \\frac{n.n-3}{1.2}A^{n-4}B^2 - \\frac{n.n-4.n-5}{1.2.3}A^{n-6}B^3 \\ldots\\ldots$\n$- nA^{n-3}C + \\frac{n.n-5}{1.2}A^{n-6}CC - \\frac{n.n-7.n-8}{1.2.3}A^{n-9}C^3 \\ldots$\nlegem generalem vide pagina sequenti",
